From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 14:10:26 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] ext4: index a block group quietly and again when the insert
 failed

mb_set_largest_free_order() and mb_update_avg_fragment_size() move a
group between the order xarrays under the group lock. The insert can only
use the atomic reserves, and when memory is that short it fails with
-ENOMEM. The code already treats that as harmless, since a group that is
not indexed is found by the linear traversal of the later criteria, but
the failed atomic allocation prints warn_alloc()'s report and stack trace
all the same, from writeback, in the middle of the pressure that caused
it:

  kworker/u19:1: page allocation failure: order:0,
      mode:0x40830(GFP_ATOMIC|__GFP_RECLAIMABLE|__GFP_COMP)
   __xas_nomem
   __xa_insert
   mb_set_largest_free_order
   mb_mark_used
   ext4_mb_regular_allocator
   ext4_writepages
   wb_workfn

The failure also loses state for longer than it needs to. Both functions
return early when the group's order is unchanged, so a group whose insert
failed stays out of its order xarray until the order changes, however
many blocks are allocated from or freed to it, and the scans that read
the xarrays for the quick criteria keep passing it over.

Insert with __GFP_NOWARN, as every caller that handles the failure does,
and let an update that finds the order unchanged check that the group is
indexed and insert it again if not. A group is missing only after a
failed insert, and the check is one lookup under the group lock.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 fs/ext4/mballoc.c | 60 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----------------
 1 file changed, 38 insertions(+), 22 deletions(-)

diff --git a/fs/ext4/mballoc.c b/fs/ext4/mballoc.c
index bef6cbb7ef74f5eabaaecd412e9efc05897f2262..0a6fa5e67d0d8678fce1cfbe6bd2e3fd0ed31293 100644
--- a/fs/ext4/mballoc.c
+++ b/fs/ext4/mballoc.c
@@ -858,6 +858,22 @@ static int mb_avg_fragment_size_order(struct super_block *sb, ext4_grpblk_t len)
 	return order;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Index @grp in @xa under its own group number. The group lock is held, so
+ * the xarray node can only come from the atomic reserves, and the insert fails
+ * when memory is that short. That is not fatal: a group that is not indexed is
+ * found by the linear traversal the later criteria fall back on, and the next
+ * update of the group's order finds it missing and indexes it again, so the
+ * failure costs a worse choice of group, not an allocation. The caller has
+ * handled it, which is why it must not warn: warn_alloc() prints a stack
+ * trace for every failed atomic allocation, and under memory pressure that
+ * adds to the very pressure that caused it.
+ */
+static int mb_index_group(struct xarray *xa, struct ext4_group_info *grp)
+{
+	return xa_insert(xa, grp->bb_group, grp, GFP_ATOMIC | __GFP_NOWARN);
+}
+
 /* Move group to appropriate avg_fragment_size list */
 static void
 mb_update_avg_fragment_size(struct super_block *sb, struct ext4_group_info *grp)
@@ -871,21 +887,21 @@ mb_update_avg_fragment_size(struct super_block *sb, struct ext4_group_info *grp)
 	old = grp->bb_avg_fragment_size_order;
 	new = grp->bb_fragments == 0 ? -1 :
 	      mb_avg_fragment_size_order(sb, grp->bb_free / grp->bb_fragments);
-	if (new == old)
-		return;
-
-	if (old >= 0)
+	if (new == old) {
+		/* Only a group whose last insert failed is missing. */
+		if (new < 0 || xa_load(&sbi->s_mb_avg_fragment_size[new],
+				       grp->bb_group))
+			return;
+	} else if (old >= 0) {
 		xa_erase(&sbi->s_mb_avg_fragment_size[old], grp->bb_group);
+	}
 
 	grp->bb_avg_fragment_size_order = new;
 	if (new >= 0) {
-		/*
-		 * Cannot use __GFP_NOFAIL because we hold the group lock.
-		 * Although allocation for insertion may fails, it's not fatal
-		 * as we have linear traversal to fall back on.
-		 */
-		int err = xa_insert(&sbi->s_mb_avg_fragment_size[new],
-				    grp->bb_group, grp, GFP_ATOMIC);
+		/* Cannot use __GFP_NOFAIL because we hold the group lock. */
+		int err = mb_index_group(&sbi->s_mb_avg_fragment_size[new],
+					 grp);
+
 		if (err)
 			mb_debug(sb, "insert group: %u to s_mb_avg_fragment_size[%d] failed, err %d",
 				 grp->bb_group, new, err);
@@ -1252,10 +1268,13 @@ mb_set_largest_free_order(struct super_block *sb, struct ext4_group_info *grp)
 			break;
 
 	/* No need to move between order lists? */
-	if (new == old)
-		return;
-
-	if (old >= 0) {
+	if (new == old) {
+		/* Only a group whose last insert failed is missing. */
+		if (new < 0 || !test_opt2(sb, MB_OPTIMIZE_SCAN) ||
+		    !grp->bb_free ||
+		    xa_load(&sbi->s_mb_largest_free_orders[new], grp->bb_group))
+			return;
+	} else if (old >= 0) {
 		struct xarray *xa = &sbi->s_mb_largest_free_orders[old];
 
 		if (!xa_empty(xa) && xa_load(xa, grp->bb_group))
@@ -1264,13 +1283,10 @@ mb_set_largest_free_order(struct super_block *sb, struct ext4_group_info *grp)
 
 	grp->bb_largest_free_order = new;
 	if (test_opt2(sb, MB_OPTIMIZE_SCAN) && new >= 0 && grp->bb_free) {
-		/*
-		 * Cannot use __GFP_NOFAIL because we hold the group lock.
-		 * Although allocation for insertion may fails, it's not fatal
-		 * as we have linear traversal to fall back on.
-		 */
-		int err = xa_insert(&sbi->s_mb_largest_free_orders[new],
-				    grp->bb_group, grp, GFP_ATOMIC);
+		/* Cannot use __GFP_NOFAIL because we hold the group lock. */
+		int err = mb_index_group(&sbi->s_mb_largest_free_orders[new],
+					 grp);
+
 		if (err)
 			mb_debug(sb, "insert group: %u to s_mb_largest_free_orders[%d] failed, err %d",
 				 grp->bb_group, new, err);
